{
  "$schema": "https://vega.github.io/schema/vega/v6.json",
  "description": "The same skewed column through all four discretizing scales, so the difference between equal widths and equal counts is visible as a row of squares. The last row is the threshold scale again with `zero: true`, which folds zero into the **cut points** rather than into a continuous extent: `[10, 50]` becomes `[0, 50]`, and every value under 10 changes bucket with it. Upstream applies `zero` by scale option and not by scale type, so a quantile and a bin-ordinal scale take it the same way, and a domain whose low end is already negative is left alone. The last two rows are the same quantize scale with and without `nice`, which upstream applies by **capability** rather than by type: of the four discretizing scales only quantize has a `nice`, inherited from the linear scale d3 builds it on, so `[8, 95]` becomes `[0, 100]` there and stays `[8, 95]` on the other three. Four of the five values change bucket with it, and the axis over each says so. A threshold scale asking for `nice: true` is the negative of that: its cut points of `[8, 57]` are the cut points it keeps, because it has no `nice` for upstream to call.",
